  "reference_protocol": "Complete statements are compared with SQLite 3.53.4 in both public file modes. Output, return code, normalized diagnostic and persisted catalog/rows/rowids/integrity must agree, including failed ALTER atomicity.",
  "implementation": "CoreSQL.prepareAddColumn factors existing name, reserved-table, duplicate and size checks out of the physical rebuild. Source generated additions reuse this actual target, validate retained declarations, reject UNIQUE/generated PRIMARY KEY constraints, then enforce empty-table storage before expression binding or row rebuilding. DROP preserves at least one ordinary column before checking surviving expression dependencies. Ordinary Core ADD behavior is unchanged.",
  "proof_scope": "The existing physical ADD theorem domain remains. The populated STORED rejection rule has a registered component contract reached by the audited production source path. A Lean runtime regression checks declaration priorities and last-ordinary protection. This is not a general proof of SQLite ALTER equivalence.",
  "feature_probe_scope": "The 88-case unittest and fresh-seed survey share named families and one generator. Their 16 failures are not a global compatibility percentage.",
